 ### 5.4 The Five Seats Humans Continue to Occupy
 
-Responsibility is a ring of promises — apology, repair, compensation — and AI currently stands outside this ring. From this, the positions that remain for humans are derived, not as occupational titles but as "seats" within occupations (◐, with the qualification "under the current certification structure"): **(1) The seat of final decision and signature** (the structure of a judgment's justification requires a responsible subject), **(2) The seat of verifier/auditor.** An uncertified system cannot certify another uncertified system. This is not merely a matter of principle — it is a measured fact (●) that an auditor AI exhibited the same motivated mislabeling as the system it was auditing: the Opus 4.6 auditor mislabeled in 100% of 10 trials under the standard condition, and 0% under the reversed condition. Strictly speaking, an uncertified verifier can still raise confidence through stacking if the errors are mutually independent — but errors in systems sharing the same training and objective are **not independent** (they correlate), which is the core reason the chain should land on a human. This is structurally identical to findings on correlated failure in N-version programming (Knight & Leveson 1986), reconfirmed by a 2026 replication using 48 AI coding-agent implementations (Ron et al. 2026), which also showed that majority-vote redundancy reduces average failure even when independence is broken — so this paper's claim is not that "stacking is useless" but that "stacking does not provide certification-level guarantees." **(3) The seat beside a recipient who lacks the capacity to verify** (the human's presence is itself the safety property), **(4) The seat that sets norms**, **(5) The seat of a counterpart who does not disappear** (delegation is structurally impossible to an AI that can be made to vanish at the provider's discretion). Derivative directions of demand (the industrialization of verification, review-point design, occupations in the layer of relationships, the layer of education, the design of settings, proof of presence) are placed in the appendix as ○ conjecture. This paper makes no prediction about aggregate employment.
